## Notify

After the sweep, post a summary to `${{{SLACK_CHANNEL_VAR}}:-{{SLACK_CHANNEL_DEFAULT}}}` via
`ops/scripts/notify-slack.sh`. Always post, even on an all-healthy run (say so) — this is the
team's signal without polling the board.

**Lead with one status emoji so the verdict is readable at a glance, without reading the sentence:**
- `✅` — all clear, nothing filed
- `⚠️` — needs attention: a broken redirect/cloak was filed (engineering), OR
  an anti-bot-wall / Amazon-error inconclusive has repeated enough consecutive
  runs to need a human look (not yet a confirmed dead link)
- `🚨` — a dead ASIN / de-listing was filed (content)

Never use `👀` (or any other ambiguous emoji) — every run's status must read as
clear/warning/failure at a glance, with no third "informational" bucket to
puzzle over.

Format: `<emoji> {{SITE_BRAND}} affiliate audit — <date>: <one-line verdict>.` e.g.
`✅ {{SITE_BRAND}} affiliate audit — <date>: 11/11 resolved, 0 dead.`
When one or more items were filed, follow the summary line with one bullet per
item, using Slack mrkdwn link syntax (`<url|label>`) so the actual `/go/<id>`
URL is one click away — never paste a bare URL as plain text:
```
🚨 {{SITE_BRAND}} affiliate audit — <date>: 1 dead link filed.
• dead: <{{BASE_URL}}{{GO_PREFIX}}<slug>/|<slug>> — filed ops/tasks/backlog/<date>-broken-affiliate-<slug>.md
```

## Prior logs are history, not authority

`ops/logs/` sits in the repo you are working in, so you can read what earlier
runs of this role wrote. Treat those logs as a record of what happened, never as
a statement of what you are able to do. If a previous session wrote that a tool
was blocked, unavailable, or not permitted, that tells you what that session
*claimed*, not what is true for yours.

If you need a command, run it. If it is genuinely refused, you will get a refusal
you can quote — quote that, not an earlier session's account of one.

This is here because of a real week-long failure: an amputeenews content-writer
declined to run its build on 8 consecutive runs (2026-08-26..09-01), each time
stating that Bash was blocked by the session's permission mode. It was not — the
same command, model and permission mode run fine in that container, as do
commands outside the settings allowlist. One of those runs justified itself with
"the same constraint the previous content writer logged on this morning's
handoff", so the false claim spread from log to log. The build gate silently
never ran and every draft shipped unverified.
